{"causes": ["The flourishing intellectual environment of Hellenistic Greece fostered diverse philosophical schools, including Epicureanism.", "Epicurus's establishment of his philosophical school in Athens attracted brilliant minds from across the Greek world, including Polyaenus.", "The growing emphasis on mathematical reasoning within ancient Greek philosophy encouraged scholars like Polyaenus to apply their expertise to new intellectual paradigms."], "effects": ["Polyaenus's mathematical expertise helped ground the early Epicurean school in rigorous intellectual inquiry.", "His close association with Epicurus contributed to the historical understanding of the Epicurean philosophical lineage.", "The intellectual exchanges between Polyaenus and Epicurus likely influenced the development of Epicurean theories on physics and cosmology."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "epicurus", "sourceName": "Epicurus", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "polyaenus-of-lampsacus", "targetName": "Polyaenus of Lampsacus", "context": "Epicurus and Polyaenus of Lampsacus were close friends and intellectual collaborators in the early Epicurean school around 300 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "polyaenus-of-lampsacus", "sourceName": "Polyaenus of Lampsacus", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "epicureanism", "targetName": "Epicureanism", "context": "Polyaenus's mathematical rigor and intellectual contributions helped shape the foundational principles of Epicureanism in the early Hellenistic period."}, {"sourceSlug": "polyaenus-of-lampsacus", "sourceName": "Polyaenus of Lampsacus", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "lampsacus", "targetName": "Lampsacus", "context": "Polyaenus was born in Lampsacus, an ancient Greek city in Asia Minor, where he likely received his early education."}, {"sourceSlug": "polyaenus-of-lampsacus", "sourceName": "Polyaenus of Lampsacus", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "hellenistic-philosophy", "targetName": "Hellenistic Philosophy", "context": "Polyaenus was an active participant in the vibrant intellectual discourse of Hellenistic Philosophy as a mathematician and Epicurean."}, {"sourceSlug": "cicero", "sourceName": "Cicero", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "polyaenus-of-lampsacus", "targetName": "Polyaenus of Lampsacus", "context": "Cicero, centuries later, referenced Polyaenus in his writings, helping to transmit knowledge of his friendship with Epicurus to subsequent generations."}], "places": [{"name": "Lampsacus, Turkey", "role": "Place of origin"}, {"name": "Athens, Greece", "role": "Center of intellectual activity"}, {"name": "Ancient Greece", "role": "Broader cultural context"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Polyaenus of Lampsacus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "polyaenus-of-lampsacus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q740432", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polyaenus_of_Lampsacus"], "tags": ["Philosophers & Thinkers", "doctrine-development", "cause-and-effect", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Polyaenus's role as a mathematician and close associate of Epicurus provided crucial intellectual rigor and legitimacy to the early Epicurean school, influencing its foundational approach to physics and cosmology. His contributions, though indirectly known, helped cement Epicureanism as a major philosophical force in the Hellenistic world.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-12T07:02:04Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29183487013", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Polyaenus of Lampsacus, also spelled Polyenus, was an ancient Greek mathematician and a friend of Epicurus.", "newValue": "Polyaenus of Lampsacus, a prominent ancient Greek mathematician and a close friend of the philosopher Epicurus, flourished in the late 4th and early 3rd centuries BCE, dying around 277 BCE. Hailing from Lampsacus, a strategically important Greek city on the Hellespont in Asia Minor, Polyaenus was a key intellectual figure in the nascent Epicurean school. His contributions primarily centered on the rigorous application of mathematics to philosophical thought, a hallmark of early Epicureanism, and his personal relationship with Epicurus cemented his place in the inner circle of one of antiquity's most enduring philosophical movements.

While Polyaenus's specific mathematical treatises have not survived independently, his intellectual legacy is evident through the accounts of later philosophers and doxographers who noted his expertise in geometry and arithmetic. He is believed to have engaged with complex mathematical problems, possibly contributing to the school's understanding of atomic theory and the infinite, areas where mathematical reasoning was crucial. His discussions with Epicurus, particularly on the nature of geometry and its relationship to physics, highlight a period of intense intellectual ferment where mathematics was not merely a tool but a foundational discipline for understanding the cosmos.

The influence of Polyaenus extended beyond his direct mathematical contributions, reinforcing the intellectual credibility of the Epicurean school during its formative years. His presence as a respected mathematician lent academic weight to Epicurus's philosophical system, particularly in an era where mathematical rigor was highly valued by rival schools like the Platonists and Peripatetics. Through his association, Polyaenus helped to establish the Epicurean emphasis on clear reasoning and empirical observation, which would profoundly shape subsequent Hellenistic philosophy and Roman thought."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-12T07:02:04Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29183487013", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "339 BCE"}, {"title": "Ongoing philosophical inquiry and intellectual debate", "type": "Idea", "year": "339 BCE"}], "newValue": ["The flourishing intellectual environment of Hellenistic Greece fostered diverse philosophical schools, including Epicureanism.", "Epicurus's establishment of his philosophical school in Athens attracted brilliant minds from across the Greek world, including Polyaenus.", "The growing emphasis on mathematical reasoning within ancient Greek philosophy encouraged scholars like Polyaenus to apply their expertise to new intellectual paradigms."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-12T07:02:04Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29183487013", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Contributed philosophical ideas to ongoing intellectual discourse", "type": "Idea", "year": "277 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "277 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Polyaenus's mathematical expertise helped ground the early Epicurean school in rigorous intellectual inquiry.", "His close associa